function computePose(keypoints, model3D) {
  const rotation = estimateRotationMatrix(keypoints, model3D);
  const translation = estimateTranslationVector(keypoints, model3D);
  return { rotation, translation };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design a practical, low-latency perception and manipulation system that must rapidly adapt to new furniture models while maintaining consistent performance across different camera configurations—a critical constraint in consumer robotics applications.

## Key Concepts
- 6-DoF pose estimation from monocular RGB
- 5-shot on-device adaptation strategies
- Cross-camera intrinsics handling techniques
- Synthetic-to-real data augmentation approaches
- Embedded GPU latency optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: estimate pose with adaptation
function estimatePoseWithAdaptation(rgbFrame, newFurnitureSamples) {
  // Detect furniture in RGB frame
  const detections = furnitureDetector.predict(rgbFrame);

  // Adapt to new furniture with 5-shot learning
  if (newFurnitureSamples.length >= 5) {
    metaLearner.adapt(newFurnitureSamples);
  }

  // Estimate 6-DoF pose using PnP
  const poses = detections.map(detection => 
    solvePnP(detection.keypoints, detection.3dModel, normalizedIntrinsics)
  );

  return { poses, detections };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design real-time non-rigid 3D reconstruction from monocular input, integrating differentiable rendering and on-device adaptation—critical for AR try-on. It also probes data strategy, evaluation, and edge-latency trade-offs in a commercial retail context.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular non-rigid 3D reconstruction with deformable garments
- Differentiable rendering and photometric consistency
- Temporal fusion for stability
- 5-shot on-device adaptation for unseen fabrics
- Edge latency and data strategy (synthetic+real)

## Code Example
```javascript
function forward(frame, params){
  const latent = encoder(frame, params.enc);
  const mesh = deformableMesh(latent, params.shape);
  const render = renderer(mesh, params.tex);
  const loss = photometric(render, frame) + temporal(mesh);
  return {mesh, loss};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle occlusion or loose fabrics?
- How evaluate mesh quality vs. latency?
- How would you construct synthetic data for textiles?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a compact computer vision pipeline with practical on-device adaptation under tight latency constraints. It also probes understanding of data strategy and evaluation beyond single-object pose tasks.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight detection with 2D bounding boxes
- Depth proxy estimation from object size
- Simple tracking with temporal consistency
- On-device few-shot adaptation (3-shot)
- Counting metrics and evaluation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: detection + tracking update loop
for (const frame of video) {
  const detections = detector.predict(frame);
  const depthProxy = estimateDepth(detections, calibration);
  const tracked = tracker.update(detections, depthProxy);
  yield { boxes: tracked, count: countPackages(tracked) };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
